# Shell functions as Ubuntu 22.04's dash (/bin/sh, 0.5.11) runs them, each case a `sh -c` from the
# login shell. What differs from bash and is recorded here: no `function` keyword, a function body
# that may be any command, `Bad function name` for a special builtin or a name that is not an
# identifier (refused before anything on the line runs), `local` that keeps a variable's value,
# `return` that outside a function ends the shell and refuses a negative or non-numeric status,
# errors that end the shell (`local` outside a function, `export -f`), the recursion limit and its
# wording, `type` that tells a special builtin from the others. Each reply was produced by
# `bash --noprofile --norc --noediting -i` started as a login shell (`exec -a -bash`) in the
# localhost/propolis-survey-ref:jammy container, run with `--network none`, PS1 and PS2 set to
# markers and these lines fed one at a time on standard input; the `sh` they run is dash 0.5.11.
# source: ubuntu:22.04 container run of dash 0.5.11 as /bin/sh (synthetic command shapes)
# date: 2026-10-09
# protocol: ssh
# persona: ubuntu
$ sh -c 'f() { echo hi; }; f; echo "rc=$?"'
> hi
> rc=0
$ sh -c 'f() { echo "n=$# 1=$1 2=$2 at=$@ zero=$0"; }; set -- x y z; f a b; echo "after n=$# 1=$1 at=$@"'
> n=2 1=a 2=b at=a b zero=sh
> after n=3 1=x at=x y z
$ sh -c 'f() { echo "$0 $1"; }; f x' name arg
> name x
$ sh -c 'f() { echo "$#"; }; f a "b c" d; f; set -- 1 2; f "$@"; f "$*"; f $*'
> 3
> 0
> 2
> 1
> 2
$ sh -c 'f() { return 3; }; f; echo "rc=$?"; g() { false; return; }; g; echo "rc=$?"; h() { true; return; }; h; echo "rc=$?"'
